{
  "slug": "postcard-mountain-skylines",
  "title": "Postcard Mountain Skylines",
  "topic": "backtracking-recursion",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "pattern": "backtracking",
  "aka": "Generate Parentheses",
  "order": 5,
  "story": [
    "<p>A designer draws simple mountain skylines for a set of postcards. The pen moves left to right in equal strokes, and each stroke goes either up a slope (<code>U</code>) or down one (<code>D</code>). A skyline starts on the horizon, ends on the horizon, and never dips below it, because the land cannot go underground. The card is short, so no point may rise more than <code>maxHeight</code> strokes above the horizon.</p>",
    "<p>For a series with a fixed number of up-strokes, the designer wants one postcard for every skyline that fits. <code>UUDD</code> is one tall peak; <code>UDUD</code> is two small hills.</p>"
  ],
  "task": [
    "<p>Write <code>allSkylines(climbs, maxHeight)</code>. Return every string made of <code>climbs</code> letters <code>U</code> and <code>climbs</code> letters <code>D</code> where, reading left to right, the height (number of <code>U</code>s so far minus number of <code>D</code>s so far) never drops below 0 and never goes above <code>maxHeight</code>.</p>",
    "<p>Return the strings in any order, each exactly once. If no skyline fits, return an empty list. For <code>climbs = 0</code> the only skyline is the flat horizon, the empty string, so return <code>[\"\"]</code>.</p>"
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "0 ≤ climbs ≤ 8",
    "0 ≤ maxHeight ≤ 8",
    "Strings may be returned in any order."
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "args": [2, 2],
      "expected": ["UUDD", "UDUD"],
      "explanation": "Two <code>U</code>s and two <code>D</code>s can be arranged 6 ways, but only 2 stay above the horizon. <code>UDDU</code> goes below after its third stroke, and <code>DUUD</code> goes below on its first. Both survivors reach at most height 2, so they fit."
    },
    {
      "args": [3, 2],
      "expected": ["UUDUDD", "UUDDUD", "UDUUDD", "UDUDUD"],
      "explanation": "Without a height limit there would be 5 skylines. The fifth, <code>UUUDDD</code>, climbs to height 3, which is taller than the card allows."
    },
    {
      "args": [0, 0],
      "expected": [""],
      "explanation": "No strokes at all. There is still one postcard, showing a flat horizon, so the answer has one empty string in it."
    }
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Write out the skylines for <code>climbs = 2</code> by hand. Which first stroke is never allowed, and why?",
    "Track the height as you draw. An up-stroke is allowed when you still have up-strokes left <em>and</em> the height is below <code>maxHeight</code>. A down-stroke is allowed only when the height is above 0. Nothing else can go wrong.",
    "So build each skyline one stroke at a time, and only ever add a stroke that is allowed. A recursive helper <code>draw(sky, upsLeft, height)</code> tries <code>U</code> and then <code>D</code>, each only when its rule allows it.",
    "When the string reaches length <code>2 × climbs</code>, record it. You do not need to check it: all up-strokes are used, so all down-strokes are too, and the height is 0. Passing <code>sky + 'U'</code> builds a new string, so there is nothing to undo after the call."
  ],
  "bruteForce": [
    "<p>Generate all 2<sup>2n</sup> strings of <code>U</code> and <code>D</code>, then keep the ones that stay between the horizon and the height limit and end on the horizon. For <code>climbs = 8</code> that is 65,536 strings checked to keep at most 1,430, and the share of waste keeps growing with n.</p>",
    "<p>Most rejected strings were doomed after a stroke or two: the ones starting with <code>D</code> alone are half of all strings. Checking at the end means building every one of them in full first.</p>"
  ],
  "signature": {
    "function": "allSkylines",
    "params": [
      {"name": "climbs", "type": "int"},
      {"name": "maxHeight", "type": "int"}
    ],
    "returns": "string[]"
  },
