  "solution": {
    "js": [
      "function allSkylines(climbs, maxHeight) {",
      "  const skylines = [];",
      "  function draw(sky, upsLeft, height) {",
      "    if (sky.length === 2 * climbs) {",
      "      skylines.push(sky); // complete, and back on the horizon",
      "      return;",
      "    }",
      "    if (upsLeft > 0 && height < maxHeight) draw(sky + 'U', upsLeft - 1, height + 1);",
      "    if (height > 0) draw(sky + 'D', upsLeft, height - 1); // stay above",
      "  }",
      "  draw('', climbs, 0);",
      "  return skylines;",
      "}"
    ],
    "py": [
      "def all_skylines(climbs, max_height):",
      "    skylines = []",
      "",
      "    def draw(sky, ups_left, height):",
      "        if len(sky) == 2 * climbs:",
      "            skylines.append(sky)  # complete, and back on the horizon",
      "            return",
      "        if ups_left > 0 and height < max_height:  # room to climb",
      "            draw(sky + 'U', ups_left - 1, height + 1)",
      "        if height > 0:            # only go down if above the horizon",
      "            draw(sky + 'D', ups_left, height - 1)",
      "",
      "    draw('', climbs, 0)",
      "    return skylines"
    ],
    "walkthrough": [
      "<p>Instead of building strings and then rejecting the bad ones, never take a stroke that breaks a rule. Two numbers decide what is allowed next: how many up-strokes are left, and the current height.</p>",
      "<p>Trace <code>climbs = 2</code>, <code>maxHeight = 2</code>. Each line is <code>(sky, upsLeft, height)</code>:</p>",
      "<ol>",
      "<li><code>(\"\", 2, 0)</code>: height is 0, so only <code>U</code> is allowed.</li>",
      "<li><code>(\"U\", 1, 1)</code>: both are allowed. Try <code>U</code> first.</li>",
      "<li><code>(\"UU\", 0, 2)</code>: no ups left (and the card's top is reached), so <code>D</code>, then <code>D</code> again. Record <code>UUDD</code>.</li>",
      "<li>Back at <code>(\"U\", 1, 1)</code>, try <code>D</code>: <code>(\"UD\", 1, 0)</code>. Height 0, so only <code>U</code>, then <code>D</code>. Record <code>UDUD</code>.</li>",
      "</ol>",
      "<p>With <code>maxHeight = 1</code> the second line would allow only <code>D</code>, and <code>UUDD</code> would never be started. As long as <code>maxHeight</code> is at least 1, every partial skyline built this way can still be finished, so no work is thrown away. The usual mistake is checking only that the counts match at the end, which lets <code>DU</code> through.</p>"
    ],
    "time": "O(4ⁿ / √n)",
    "timeWhy": "The number of skylines (the Catalan numbers) grows like 4ⁿ / n^1.5, each takes O(n) to build, and no dead-end string is ever started.",
    "space": "O(n)",
    "spaceWhy": "The recursion is 2n calls deep, not counting the output.",
    "takeaway": "Prune while you build: carry the few numbers that decide which next step is legal (here, ups left and the height, checked against both limits) and only recurse on legal steps. Balanced brackets, valid stack orders and many \"well-formed string\" problems use the same two counters."
  },
  "related": ["backtracking", "recursion"]
